### Calculation

First, calculate the molar mass of the starting material, 4-nitrotoluene (\(C_{7}H_{7}NO_{2}\)).
\text{Molar mass} = (7 \times 12) + (7 \times 1) + (1 \times 14) + (2 \times 16) = 84 + 7 + 14 + 32 = 137 \text{ g mol}^{-1}
Next, calculate the moles of 4-nitrotoluene used.
\text{Mole} = \frac{\text{Given mass}}{\text{Molar mass}} = \frac{137 \times 10^{-3} \text{ g}}{137 \text{ g mol}^{-1}} = 0.001 \text{ mole}
Assuming the reaction goes to completion, the stoichiometry between the starting material and the final product (B) is 1:1. Therefore, 0.001 moles of product B will be formed.
\text{Mole of product} = 0.001 \text{ mole}
Now, calculate the molar mass of the final product B, N-(2-bromo-4-methylphenyl)acetamide (\(C_{9}H_{10}BrNO\)).
\text{Molar mass of B} = (9 \times 12) + (10 \times 1) + (1 \times 80) + (1 \times 14) + (1 \times 16) = 108 + 10 + 80 + 14 + 16 = 228 \text{ g mol}^{-1}
Finally, calculate the mass of product B formed.
\text{Mass of product} = \text{Moles} \times \text{Molar mass} = 0.001 \text{ mole} \times 228 \text{ g mol}^{-1}
= 0.228 \text{ g} = 228 \text{ mg}

The basicity of amines depends on the availability of the lone pair of electrons on the nitrogen atom. Electron-donating groups increase basicity, while electron-withdrawing groups decrease basicity.

### Comparing Aliphatic Amines (E and D)

For aliphatic amines, the basicity is primarily governed by the \(+I\) effect of alkyl groups. More alkyl groups lead to a stronger \(+I\) effect, increasing electron density on nitrogen and thus increasing basicity.
\text{(E) } (CH_{3})_{2}NH \text{ (dimethylamine) has two } CH_{3} \text{ groups (more +I effect)}
\text{(D) } CH_{3}NH_{2} \text{ (methylamine) has one } CH_{3} \text{ group (less +I effect)}
Therefore, \(E > D\).

### Comparing Aromatic Amines (A, B, C) and Aliphatic Amines

Aromatic amines are generally less basic than aliphatic amines because the lone pair on nitrogen is delocalized into the benzene ring via resonance, making it less available for protonation.
\text{(A) Aniline: Lone pair is delocalized.}
\text{(B) p-Methoxyaniline: The } -OCH_{3} \text{ group is electron-donating via the mesomeric effect (+M effect) and electron-withdrawing via the inductive effect (-I effect). The +M effect dominates, increasing electron density on nitrogen and thus increasing basicity compared to aniline.}
\text{(C) p-Nitroaniline: The } -NO_{2} \text{ group is strongly electron-withdrawing via both the mesomeric effect (-M effect) and inductive effect (-I effect). This significantly decreases electron density on nitrogen, making it the least basic.}
So, the order for aromatic amines is \(B > A > C\).

### Overall Order of Basicity

Combining the aliphatic and aromatic amines, the overall descending order of basicity is:
E > D > B > A > C
This corresponds to option (2).

