The reaction involves the anti-Markovnikov addition of HBr to an alkene, followed by nucleophilic substitution. The steps are as follows:

### Step-by-step Calculation

1. Calculate the moles of the reactant P (4-methyloct-1-ene, \(C_{9}H_{18}\)).
\text{Molar mass of P} = (9 \times 12) + (18 \times 1) = 108 + 18 = 126 \text{ g/mol}
\text{Moles of P} = \frac{\text{Given mass}}{\text{Molar mass}} = \frac{2.52 \text{ g}}{126 \text{ g/mol}} = 0.02 \text{ mol}
2. The combined yield of the two isomeric bromides is 50%.
\text{Total moles of bromides formed} = 0.02 \text{ mol} \times 50\% = 0.01 \text{ mol}
3. The primary (1°) and secondary (2°) bromides are formed in a 9:1 ratio. The primary bromide is the major product due to the anti-Markovnikov mechanism.
\text{Moles of primary bromide} = 0.01 \text{ mol} \times \frac{9}{10} = 0.009 \text{ mol}
4. The entire amount of the primary bromide is converted to product S with 100% yield.
\text{Moles of S formed} = \text{Moles of primary bromide} = 0.009 \text{ mol}
5. Calculate the molar mass of product S (N,N-diethyl-4-methyloctan-1-amine, \(C_{13}H_{29}N\)).
\text{Molar mass of S} = (13 \times 12) + (29 \times 1) + (1 \times 14) = 156 + 29 + 14 = 199 \text{ g/mol}
6. Calculate the mass of S obtained.
\text{Mass of S} = \text{moles} \times \text{molar mass} = 0.009 \text{ mol} \times 199 \text{ g/mol} = 1.791 \text{ g}
7. Convert the mass to milligrams (mg).
\text{Mass of S in mg} = 1.791 \text{ g} \times 1000 \frac{\text{mg}}{\text{g}} = 1791 \text{ mg}

